## 2026-07-27 - US-002

Delete the telemetry module and every one of its call sites.

**Interpretation:** a pure deletion story with no unit test runner in
the repo yet (that arrives in US-019), so per execution rule 3 no
failing test was written first. The behavioural claim that *can* be
falsified -- "uncaught errors still surface in the devtools console" --
was verified empirically instead; see Verification below.

**What was implemented**

- Deleted `src/renderer/lib/telemetry.js` (222 lines).
- Removed all 7 call sites in `src/renderer/main.js`: the import,
  `telemetry.init(state)`, the capturing `window.addEventListener
  ('error', ...)` handler, `telemetry.send(state)` in `delayedInit`,
  the 12-hour `setInterval` send, and the `wt-uncaught-error`
  ipcRenderer handler.
- The `uncaughtError` dispatcher action is kept as a
  `console.error` no-op rather than removed, because
  `src/main/index.js:98` still calls
  `windows.main.dispatch('uncaughtError', 'main', error)`. Removing it
  would make the dispatcher fail to resolve that action by name, and it
  also keeps main-process errors visible in the renderer console.
- `controllers/media-controller.js`: import plus all three
  `logPlayAttempt` calls removed. `mediaSuccess()` had no other body,
  so it is now an empty method with a comment -- the dispatcher
  resolves actions by name, so it must still exist.
- `controllers/playback-controller.js`: import plus the
  `logPlayAttempt('abandoned')` call removed.
- `TELEMETRY_URL` removed from `src/config.js`.
- Added a "Resolved by deletion (US-002)" section to
  `docs/vite-baseline.md` recording which inventory entries this
  deletion retires and the revised first-party counts.

**Files changed**

- `src/renderer/lib/telemetry.js` (deleted)
- `src/renderer/main.js`
- `src/renderer/controllers/media-controller.js`
- `src/renderer/controllers/playback-controller.js`
- `src/config.js`
- `docs/vite-baseline.md`
- `specs/prd.json`, `progress.log`

**Verification**

- `grep -rn "telemetry" src/` returns nothing (exit 1).
- Nothing outside `telemetry.js` ever read `state.saved.telemetry`,
  confirmed by grep across `src/` and `test/` before deleting. No
  migration needed; the stale key just sits unread in existing users'
  saved state.
- `npm test` (standard + depcheck) passes, exit 0. No dependency was
  orphaned: `arch` is still used by `src/config.js`, `simple-get` by
  `main/updater.js` and `main/announcement.js`.
- `npm run build` (Babel) compiles 64 files, was 65.
- App launches via `npx electron --no-sandbox .`: `init: 703ms`, both
  windows up.
- **Uncaught errors still reach the renderer console -- measured, not
  assumed.** See the technique in Codebase Patterns below. The probe
  printed `RENDERER-CONSOLE: [3,"Uncaught Error: US-002 uncaught error
  probe",...]`, severity 3 = error. Build artifacts were regenerated
  with `npm run build` afterwards.
- NOT run: the six-step manual smoke test, which needs an interactive
  operator. Step 3 (video playback) is the one at risk here, since both
  media-controller and playback-controller were edited. The removed
  calls were all pure fire-and-forget counters with no return value
  consumed, so the risk is low, but this is an untested claim.

**Learnings for future iterations:**

- **`electron.remote` is already broken on master.** The probe caught
  `Uncaught (in promise) TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined
  (reading 'getCurrentWindow')` from `main.js:168`, present before this
  change (`git show HEAD:src/renderer/main.js` has the same line) and
  untouched by it. `electron.remote` was removed from the `electron`
  module in modern Electron; the working form is
  `require('@electron/remote')`, which is what `path-selector.js` and
  `torrent-list-controller.js` already do. Three call sites in
  `main.js` (lines 168, 429, 441) still use the dead form. US-024 and
  US-027 will have to fix this while building the window shim -- do not
  mistake it for a regression introduced by shim work.
- **The dispatcher resolves actions by name at call time**, so deleting
  the last statement out of an action handler is not the same as
  deleting the handler. `main/index.js` and the JSX components both
  dispatch by string. Check for remaining dispatchers before removing
  an action.
- **`NOTES/architecture.md` and `NOTES/product.md` do not exist** in
  this repo, despite the standing session instructions naming them. The
  real context is `specs/prd.json`, `progress.log`,
  `tasks/prd-renderer-decoupling.md`, and `docs/vite-baseline.md`.
  Don't waste a turn looking for `NOTES/`.

## 2026-07-27 - US-003

Remove the `Module.prototype.require` monkey patch from the renderer
entry point and replace it with a Vite `resolve.alias`.

**Interpretation:** per execution rule 3 this is a
config/refactor story with no behavioural delta to assert, and the repo
still has no unit test runner (US-019). Instead of a test, I wrote two
throwaway *empirical probes* and kept their output as the evidence --
see Verification below.

**What was implemented**

- Deleted the `require('module')` import and the
  `Module.prototype.require` override at the top of
  `src/renderer/main.js`. Nothing else in the file used `Module`.
- Added `stubs/inline-style-prefixer.js`, a committed no-op module that
  `export default {}` -- exactly the fake object the old hook returned.
- Added `resolve.alias` to `vite.config.js` mapping
  `inline-style-prefixer` to that stub.

**Files changed**

- `src/renderer/main.js`
- `vite.config.js`
- `stubs/inline-style-prefixer.js` (new)
- `specs/prd.json` (US-003 `passes: true`)
- `progress.log`

**Verification**

- Alias probe (throwaway `.probe/` entry importing both the bare
  specifier and `inline-style-prefixer/static/createPrefixer`, built
  with and without the alias): with the alias 9 modules / 6.16 kB,
  without it 40 modules / 75.36 kB, and `createPrefixer` is still
  present in the aliased bundle. So the alias fires on the bare
  specifier and does *not* break the deep imports.
- `npm run build` (Babel) compiles 64 files.
- App launches via `npx electron --no-sandbox .`, reaches
  `init: 789ms`, both windows up.
- Render probe, before vs after, on the torrent list page and the
  preferences page: vendor-prefixed inline-style count and
  `.header` outerHTML length are byte-identical
  (home 0 / 463, prefs 14 / 396 in both runs).
- `npm test` (standard + depcheck) passes, exit 0.
- No CSS file touched.
- **The six-step manual smoke test was NOT run** -- this session is
  non-interactive. The steps that carry residual risk are the ones the
  probe cannot reach: playing a torrent and the create-torrent flow.
  Neither touches material-ui theming, so the risk is low.

**Learnings for future iterations:**

- **The old hook was already dead code for its stated purpose.**
  Nothing in `node_modules` does `require('inline-style-prefixer')`
  bare -- material-ui only imports the deep paths
  (`inline-style-prefixer/static/...`, `.../dynamic/...`). So the
  ~40ms it claimed to save was never actually being saved. Removing it
  changes nothing at runtime, which is why the before/after render
  probes match.
- **Alias the bare specifier with a regex, not a string.** Vite alias
  string `find` entries match as a *prefix*, so
  `find: 'inline-style-prefixer'` would rewrite
  `inline-style-prefixer/static/createPrefixer` to
  `stubs/inline-style-prefixer.js/static/createPrefixer` and fail to
  resolve. `find: /^inline-style-prefixer$/` is the correct form.
- **Vite probes must live inside the repo.** A probe entry file under
  `/tmp` cannot resolve the project's `node_modules`; Rolldown reports
  it as an unresolved-import error that looks like a real bug. Put
  throwaway probe configs in a gitignored dir at the repo root instead.
- **Home-page DOM metrics are noisy between runs.** Torrent progress
  bars add inline styles as downloads advance, so `bodyLen` and a raw
  `[style]` count differ run to run by ~20%. For before/after render
  comparisons use stable anchors: the `.header` outerHTML and the
  preferences page, which were identical across every run.
- **`window.dispatch` and `window.state` are exposed by
  `src/renderer/main.js`**, which makes non-interactive route
  navigation possible from the main process:
  `wc.executeJavaScript("window.dispatch('preferences')")`. The
  webtorrent hidden window also fires `did-finish-load`, so a
  `web-contents-created` probe reports twice -- the one with
  `bodyLen: 53` and no `.header` is the hidden window, not a failure.
